// High Number of SSM Parameters Retrieval (T1555.006)
//
// Two signals, and the second is the one that matters:
//   1. A recursive, decrypting GetParametersByPath — an entire hierarchy drained
//      in one call, with the parameter names absent from the event.
//   2. The count of DISTINCT parameters actually decrypted, taken from the
//      PARAMETER_ARN encryption context on each kms:Decrypt rather than from the
//      number of ssm API calls. GetParameters accepts 10 names per call and
//      GetParametersByPath returns up to 10 parameters per page, so an SSM event
//      count under-reports the secrets exposed by up to a factor of ten.
//
// CloudTrail records the parameter NAME and never the VALUE. That is enough to
// enumerate exactly what was taken and rotate precisely — a blanket rotation is
// not required and is usually the wrong response.
//
// Field casing: the SSM API Reference documents the wire format (WithDecryption,
// Recursive); AWS's published ssm.amazonaws.com CloudTrail examples render request
// keys with a lower-case initial. Both spellings are read below so the query does
// not silently return nothing if your trail renders the other form.
//
// Dialect: Sentinel / Azure Log Analytics KQL. NOT CloudWatch Logs Insights.
AWSCloudTrail
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where EventSource == "ssm.amazonaws.com"
| where EventName in ("GetParameter", "GetParameters", "GetParametersByPath")
| where isempty(ErrorCode)
| extend Req = parse_json(RequestParameters)
| extend WithDecryption = tobool(coalesce(Req.withDecryption, Req.WithDecryption, false))
| extend Recursive      = tobool(coalesce(Req.recursive, Req.Recursive, false))
| extend TargetPath     = tostring(coalesce(Req.path, Req.Path, ""))
| extend TargetName     = tostring(coalesce(Req.name, Req.Name, ""))
| extend NameCount      = array_length(todynamic(coalesce(Req.names, Req.Names, dynamic([]))))
| extend ParametersTouched = case(
    EventName == "GetParameter",         1,
    EventName == "GetParameters",        iff(NameCount > 0, NameCount, 1),
    10)                                          // ByPath: MaxResults ceiling, a floor on truth
| where WithDecryption
| summarize
    Calls           = count(),
    PathDrains      = countif(EventName == "GetParametersByPath" and Recursive),
    MinParameters   = sum(ParametersTouched),    // LOWER BOUND, see note below
    Paths           = make_set_if(TargetPath, isnotempty(TargetPath), 10),
    Names           = make_set_if(TargetName, isnotempty(TargetName), 25),
    SourceIPs       = make_set(SourceIpAddress, 5),
    Agents          = make_set(UserAgent, 3),
    Regions         = make_set(AwsRegion, 5),
    FirstSeen       = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen        = max(TimeGenerated)
    by UserIdentityArn, bin(TimeGenerated, 5m)
| where MinParameters >= 30 or PathDrains > 0
| extend Verdict = case(
    PathDrains > 0,
        "RECURSIVE DECRYPTED PATH DRAIN — names are NOT in the event, reconstruct from the path, P0",
    MinParameters >= 100,
        "BULK SECURESTRING DECRYPTION — treat every named parameter as disclosed, P0",
    "ELEVATED DECRYPTED READ VOLUME — confirm the caller is a configuration loader, P1")
| project FirstSeen, LastSeen, UserIdentityArn, Calls, MinParameters, PathDrains,
          Paths, Names, SourceIPs, Agents, Regions, Verdict
| order by LastSeen desc
//
// ============================================================================
// THE COUNT THAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE — distinct parameters decrypted
// ============================================================================
// MinParameters above is a lower bound: for GetParametersByPath the response is
// not logged, so the query assumes the MaxResults ceiling of 10 per call. The
// KMS side gives the exact set, one Decrypt per SecureString value, each naming
// its parameter. Run this to size the incident and to build the rotation list.
//
// AWSCloudTrail
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
// | where EventSource == "kms.amazonaws.com" and EventName == "Decrypt"
// | where isempty(ErrorCode)
// | extend Ctx = parse_json(RequestParameters).encryptionContext
// | extend ParameterArn = tostring(Ctx.PARAMETER_ARN)
// | where ParameterArn contains ":parameter/"
// | summarize
//     DistinctParameters = dcount(ParameterArn),
//     ParameterArns      = make_set(ParameterArn, 200),
//     KeyIds             = make_set(tostring(parse_json(RequestParameters).keyId), 5),
//     FirstSeen          = min(TimeGenerated),
//     LastSeen           = max(TimeGenerated)
//     by UserIdentityArn, bin(TimeGenerated, 5m)
// | where DistinctParameters >= 30
// | order by DistinctParameters desc
//
// ParameterArns is the rotation work-list. Every ARN in it had its plaintext
// returned to the caller.
//
// ============================================================================
// WHAT NEITHER QUERY CAN SEE
// ============================================================================
// GetParameter on a name that does not exist is NOT logged at all: AWS documents
// that "For the DeleteParameter and GetParameter actions, if the specified
// parameter doesn't exist, the ParameterNotFound exception is not recorded in AWS
// CloudTrail event logs." Name-guessing through GetParameter is therefore
// invisible. GetParameters (plural) does the opposite — unknown names come back in
// the 200-response InvalidParameters list with no ErrorCode — so enumeration
// through the plural call looks like clean success. Neither shape can be
// distinguished by an error filter; the signal is a caller whose read set has an
// unusually low hit rate against DescribeParameters, which has to be computed
// against the parameter inventory rather than against the log.
//
// IAM DENY BYPASS, DOCUMENTED: "if a user has access to a path, then the user can
// access all levels of that path... Even if a user has explicitly been denied
// access in IAM for parameter /a/b, they can still call the GetParametersByPath
// API operation recursively for /a and view /a/b." A per-parameter Deny is not a
// containment control. Scope the Allow on the path instead.
//
// The aws/ssm AWS managed key cannot carry a key policy of its own, so an
// encryption-context condition on kms:Decrypt is only enforceable when the
// parameters use a customer managed key.
